Fix the Objective-C layer so it compiles. Written at 2b363be4 (0.16.0-dev on
main); still needed at 83de55a1 (0.17.1).

Four defects, all of which stop `src/objectivec` building for iOS. Together they
say upstream does not compile this layer in CI at either commit.

1. `ort_genai_objc.h` declares `processImages:images:error:`,
   `processAudios:audios:error:` and `processImagesAndAudios:images:audios:error:`
   twice each, once taking `NSString*` and once `NSArray<NSString*>*`. An
   Objective-C selector does not carry parameter types, so the two forms are one
   selector and the second is a duplicate declaration. The array-taking forms are
   renamed to `…WithPrompts:` rather than deleted, so the capability survives.

2. `oga_multi_modal_processor.mm` reads `prompt_strings` where the surrounding
   code declares `prompts_strings`.

3. `oga_tokenizer.mm` assigns `Tokenizer::GetEosTokenIds()` to a
   `std::vector<int32_t>`, but under C++20 the C++ core returns
   `std::span<const int32_t>` (`ort_genai.h`, `OGA_USE_SPAN`). The Objective-C
   layer is out of step with the core it ships beside.

4. Three methods carry `NS_SWIFT_NAME(name())` while taking an `NSError**`, so the
   Swift name declares no parameter for a method that has one. Clang rejects each.
   The parameter is added to the attribute rather than the attribute removed, which
   keeps the Swift spelling upstream evidently wanted.

Retire this whole patch on a version bump that fixes them upstream; if it stops
applying, that is the signal to check.

